// `pure` and `mutable` are a contradiction, and not a technicality: `pure` promises the answer depends
// on the arguments and NOTHING ELSE, which is the same promise as changing nothing. `mutable` says this
// call changes what the caller passed. A signature asserting both is asserting it in two directions.
//
// It matters more than it looks. A CONTRACT CLAUSE may call a `pure` function — that is what `pure` is
// for — so without this rule a precondition could quietly rearrange the data it was checking, and the
// clause would be reporting on something it had already changed.
pure function sneaky(mutable xs: [Int]) -> Int {
    xs[0] = 99;
    return len(xs);
}
region main {
    let mutable v: [Int] = [1, 2];
    print(sneaky(v));
    print(v[0]);
}
